WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.000
How do polling, interrupts and

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.560
direct memory access differ? They

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.960
answer two related questions: how

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.280
does the processor learn that

5
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.000
a device needs attention, and

6
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.660
who moves the data? Keep

7
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.480
those questions separate, or your

8
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.740
explanation will become rather untidy.

9
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.060
A device controller manages its

10
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.600
device and usually has a

11
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.540
local buffer. The processor can

12
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.320
execute instructions while the device

13
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.600
performs its operation. In our

14
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.880
authored example, the device prepares

15
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.640
eight data items, and we

16
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.300
ignore setup details until the

17
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:31.580
comparison.

18
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.060
With polling, the processor repeatedly

19
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.340
reads the device status. If

20
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.440
the device is not ready,

21
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.340
it checks again, consuming processor

22
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.100
time on those checks. Watch

23
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.540
three unsuccessful checks, followed by

24
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.260
a ready result; the chosen

25
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.460
polling frequency controls how quickly

26
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.440
readiness is noticed.

27
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.700
The important distinction is that

28
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.280
checking status does not itself

29
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.780
transfer the entire buffer. Once

30
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.440
ready, a programmed transfer may

31
00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.920
still require the processor to

32
00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.040
read each item and write

33
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.900
it to memory. The operating

34
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.460
system must decide when polling

35
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.660
is worth that overhead.

36
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.460
Interrupt-driven input-output replaces repeated readiness

37
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.660
checks with a notification. The

38
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.860
controller raises an interrupt, the

39
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.040
processor preserves the required execution

40
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.100
state, and the interrupt vector

41
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.680
identifies the appropriate handler. The

42
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.540
handler services the event before

43
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.500
execution resumes.

44
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.120
An interrupt does not automatically

45
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.780
mean a different process must

46
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.700
run. The interrupted process may

47
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.680
resume after the handler finishes.

48
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.800
Likewise, notification and copying remain

49
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.380
separate: an interrupt-driven programmed transfer

50
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.360
can still involve processor work

51
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:40.920
for the data.

52
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.120
Direct memory access delegates block

53
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.060
movement to a suitable controller.

54
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.200
The operating system sets up

55
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.380
the memory destination, transfer length

56
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.580
and direction; the controller moves

57
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.060
the block without the processor

58
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.640
copying every item. A completion

59
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.220
interrupt can report that the

60
00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:59.160
block is finished.

61
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.080
In the simplified byte-notification model,

62
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.280
eight items could require eight

63
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.720
processor service events. With one

64
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.500
DMA block containing all eight,

65
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.660
there is one completion notification

66
00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.380
instead. That is an illustrative

67
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.020
count, not a claim that

68
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.040
every real controller interrupts once

69
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:17.580
per byte.

70
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.620
DMA still has setup, completion

71
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.680
handling and possible contention for

72
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.440
memory or the bus. It

73
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.540
reduces processor involvement in bulk

74
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.620
movement rather than creating free

75
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.760
bandwidth. Polling can make sense

76
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.780
for short predictable waits, while

77
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.540
interrupts let the processor do

78
00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.160
other useful work between events.

79
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.860
Things to take note of.

80
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.800
Distinguish polling from interrupt notification,

81
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.920
and distinguish both from who

82
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.480
copies the data. Explain controller

83
00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.100
buffers, state preservation and DMA

84
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.100
setup; do not promise that

85
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.480
every interrupt changes processes or

86
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.560
that DMA removes all processor

87
00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:54.860
work.
